LightPreset: write a colorAniList element with r == g == b as a greyscale scalar.

`colorAniList` is one string of ASCII colour tokens and ZenGin writes it in two
forms — a bare greyscale scalar (`255 `) for a colour whose channels are equal,
a parenthesized triple (`(64 128 128) `) for one whose channels differ. The
ABNF comment in `LightPreset::load` above states exactly that, and the loader
accepts both; `LightPreset::save` then re-emitted every element as a triple,
because the parsed `std::vector<Color>` no longer carries which form it came
from. So a world that stored a greyscale element could not be re-saved
byte-identically — the only field a byte-diff of the retail corpus still
reported.

The channel values are the fact that decides the form. Measured over the three
retail Gothic II worlds (NewWorld, OldWorld, AddonWorld) by walking the
`colorAniList` BinSafe string entries directly: of 5,240 elements in 1,111
strings, 26 are stored as greyscale scalars (8 / 2 / 16) and NONE of the 5,214
triples has r == g == b. Emitting short exactly when r == g == b therefore
re-emits all 1,111 retail strings byte-for-byte, and needs no public API and no
retained original text.

diff --git a/src/vobs/Light.cc b/src/vobs/Light.cc
index f2d8201..ce5d7eb 100644
--- a/src/vobs/Light.cc
+++ b/src/vobs/Light.cc
@@ -104,8 +104,17 @@ namespace zenkit {
 			os.str("");
 			os.clear();
 			for (auto& c : this->color_animation_list) {
-				os << "(" << static_cast<int>(c.r) << " " << static_cast<int>(c.g) << " " << static_cast<int>(c.b)
-				   << ") ";
+				// ZenGin writes an element whose channels are equal as a bare greyscale
+				// scalar and only a differing one as a triple, and the loader above
+				// accepts both. Always emitting triples loses the shorthand: measured
+				// over the retail Gothic II worlds, 26 of the 5,240 elements are stored
+				// short and none of the 5,214 triples has r == g == b.
+				if (c.r == c.g && c.g == c.b) {
+					os << static_cast<int>(c.r) << " ";
+				} else {
+					os << "(" << static_cast<int>(c.r) << " " << static_cast<int>(c.g) << " "
+					   << static_cast<int>(c.b) << ") ";
+				}
 			}
 			w.write_string("colorAniList", os.str());
 
